//! Compile-time questions about a type: its name, its kind, its fields, an enum's variants, the
//! interfaces it conforms to.
//!
//! Each answer is a constant of the type it is asked of — in a generic function or a default, of
//! each instance — and there is no reflection at run time: what a program reads of a type it
//! reads as such a constant.
//!
//! A `comptime for (f in fields<T>())` sets its body in place once per field, where `e.[f]` is
//! the field `f` names, of the field's own type; one over `variants<E>()` once per variant, where
//! `e is v` tests the variant, `E.[v]` is a unit variant's value and `e.[v, p]` a payload field;
//! one over `members<T>()` once per member. A field is listed where the code that asks may name
//! it: an interface of another package sees the `pub` fields alone. What a declaration carries,
//! a round reads: `attribute<A>(f)` of a field — a payload's too —, `variantAttribute<A>(v)` of a
//! variant, `memberAttribute<A>(m)` of a member — a function, a constant, an associated type —,
//! `parameterAttribute<A>(p)` of a parameter in a round over `m.parameters`, and
//! `typeAttribute<T, A>()` of a type, an interface or an alias. What stands at a module's top
//! level a macro reads through `std.syntax`.
//!
//! The questions and the infos are declared in `std.core`, whose synthesized members are written
//! over them and which imports nothing: this module is their name to the program.
pub import std.core {
    Visibility,
    TypeKind,
    Type,
    FieldInfo,
    VariantInfo,
    MemberInfo,
    MemberKind,
    ParameterInfo,
    fields,
    members,
    attribute,
    variantAttribute,
    memberAttribute,
    parameterAttribute,
    typeAttribute,
    variants,
    conformances,
    hasConformance,
    typeName,
    isStruct,
    isClass,
    isEnum,
    isInterface,
};
